course: technology
lesson: 11
title: Cooking machines: oven, microwave, kettle, toaster and hob
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every cooking machine in the kitchen does one job: it turns electricity or burning gas into heat and puts that heat into food.
explain: They do it in different ways: the oven heats the air in a box, the toaster makes thin wires glow red, the kettle has a hot metal element sitting in the water, and the microwave makes the water inside the food wiggle.
explain: Many of them have a clever little switch that decides when to stop, like the click you hear when the kettle has boiled.
explain: Think of Sunday morning: the kettle clicks off, the toast pops up, and the oven ticks as it holds its heat, all without anyone watching a clock.
explain: The heat that cooks food also burns skin, so the rule is simple: the hot jobs belong to grown-ups, and you keep your hands and face away.
fact: An oven heats with glowing electric elements or a gas flame, and a thermostat switches the heat on and off to hold the temperature you chose; a fan oven blows the hot air round.
fact: A microwave oven sends out invisible microwaves that make the water in food wiggle and heat up, and the turntable spins so the food heats more evenly; microwaves heat only the outer few centimetres of the food, and the heat then spreads inwards.
fact: The metal mesh in a microwave door has holes too small for microwaves to pass through but big enough for light, so you can see in safely, and the microwaves stop as soon as the door opens.
fact: A kettle's switch has a strip made of two metals that bend differently when steam warms them, so it clicks off by itself when the water boils.
fact: The white crust inside a kettle is limescale, minerals left behind from hard water.
fact: A toaster's thin wires glow red hot, and the bread turns brown through the same kind of reaction that browns cooked meat.
fact: Gas hobs burn gas in rings of small flames, electric hobs heat with elements, and an induction hob uses a magnetic field to heat the pan itself, so the glass stays cooler than the pan.
fact: An extractor fan sucks up steam and smoke through a filter, and greasy filters must be cleaned because the grease can catch fire.
fact: An air fryer does not really fry: it is a small fan oven that blows very hot air round the food so the outside crisps with little oil.
fact: A slow cooker heats a pot gently for many hours, using about as much power as a light bulb or two, and stays hot long after it is switched off.
fact: When the knob of a gas cooker is turned, a spark lights the gas; natural gas has no smell of its own, so a smell is added to make leaks easy to notice.
fact: If you smell gas: tell an adult at once, do not press any switches, and get out; the adults open windows and call for help.
fact: The story goes that the microwave oven was invented in the 1940s when an engineer working with a radar machine noticed it had melted a sweet in his pocket.
fact: Never put metal or foil in a microwave, and never poke a knife or fork into a toaster, even when it is switched off.
exercise: How does the oven keep the temperature you chose? | A thermostat switches the heat on and off | The oven guesses from the clock | The fan blows cold air in all the time | The door lets heat out when it is too hot
exercise: How does a microwave oven heat food? | It makes the water in the food wiggle and heat up | It shines a very hot light bulb straight onto the food | It blows hot air from a hidden heater all round the food | It heats the plate first, and then the plate heats the food
exercise: Why does the turntable in a microwave spin round? | So the food heats more evenly | So the microwaves can get out | So the plate stays cold | So the bell knows when to ring
exercise: Why can you see into a microwave but the microwaves cannot get out? | The mesh holes are too small for microwaves but not for light | The glass in the door is bulletproof | The microwaves only travel downwards | The light inside pushes the microwaves back
exercise: How does a kettle know when to switch itself off? | Steam bends a strip of two metals that clicks the switch | A tiny clock inside counts three minutes and then stops it | The water gets heavy and presses a button | The handle feels the heat and lets go
exercise: What is the white crust inside a kettle? | Limescale, minerals left behind from hard water | Dried soap from washing it up | Rust from the metal element | Salt that people put in the water
exercise: Why does toast turn brown and crunchy? | Heat causes a browning reaction, like in cooked meat | The wires paint it with brown crumbs | The bread is burned black first and then cools down to brown | The bread soaks up smoke from the wires
exercise: How does an induction hob heat a pan? | A magnetic field heats the pan itself | A gas flame hides under the glass | Hot water runs under the glass top | The glass glows red and heats the pan
exercise: What does an extractor fan over the cooker do? | Sucks up steam and smoke through a filter | Blows cold air onto the food | Lights the gas on the hob | Keeps the oven at the right heat
exercise: Why must the greasy filter of an extractor fan be cleaned? | The grease can catch fire | It makes the fan spin backwards | It turns the food greasy | It makes the cooker smell of gas
exercise: What does an air fryer really do? | Blows very hot air round the food, like a small fan oven | Fries the food in a deep pool of oil | Heats the food with microwaves | Freezes the food and then thaws it fast
exercise: How can a slow cooker cook all day without burning the food? | It heats the pot gently, not fiercely | It switches itself off for a rest every ten minutes | It adds a little water to the food all by itself | It cools the bottom of the pot with a small fan
exercise: What makes the click and the flame on a gas cooker? | A spark from the igniter lights the gas | A tiny match strikes inside the knob | The gas lights itself when it meets air | The click is a fan starting up
exercise: Why does gas from the cooker smell, when natural gas has no smell? | A smell is added so leaks are noticed | Gas only starts to smell once it gets old | The smell comes from the pipes | Only bad gas has a smell
exercise: You smell gas in the kitchen. What should you do first? | Tell an adult at once and press no switches | Turn on the light so you can look for the leak | Light a match to check the gas | Switch the cooker on and off again
exercise: What must never go inside a microwave oven? | Metal or foil | A glass bowl | A plastic microwave lid | A mug of water
exercise: Mum has a hob with 4 rings and a toaster with 2 slots. How many things can heat at once? | 6 | 4 | 8 | 2
test: my bro says microwaves cook food from the inside out. true? | No, they heat the outer few centimetres and the heat spreads in | Yes, the middle always gets hot first | Yes, that is why the outside stays cold | No, they only warm the plate
test: In a cartoon a cat pokes a fork into the toaster to get the bread out. What is the real rule? | Never poke anything in a toaster; an adult unplugs it first | Use a metal knife instead of a fork | It is fine to reach in once the toast has popped up and cooled | Just do it quickly so it doesn't hurt
test: ugh the kettle's broken, it keeps clicking off on its own. is it? | No, it clicks off when the water boils, on purpose | Yes, kettles should keep boiling until unplugged | Yes, the click means a fuse inside has blown and needs changing | No, it clicks off to save the water
test: Grandad wrapped his sandwich in foil and wants to microwave it. What do you say? | Take the foil off; metal must not go in a microwave | Foil is fine, it keeps the heat in | Only foil with holes in it is safe | Wrap it twice so it heats faster
test: What would happen if the microwave door stayed working while it was open? | It doesn't: safety switches stop the microwaves when it opens | The kitchen would fill with lovely smells and nothing else would happen | The microwaves would turn into light | Nothing, the turntable would just stop
test: Ella reckons her air fryer fries chips in oil like a chip shop. Right? | No, it bakes them with fast hot air | Yes, it is full of boiling oil hidden inside | Yes, but the oil is invisible so you can't see it | No, it cooks them with microwaves like an oven
test: why's the plate hot if microwaves heat the food?? | The hot food warms the plate it sits on | Microwaves like plates more than they like the food | The turntable rubs against it as it turns and heats it | The little light in the microwave heats it up
test: Tom touched the black glass of an induction hob next to a pan and it was only warm. Why? | The magnetic field heats the pan, not the glass | The hob was switched off all along | Glass can never get hot | Induction hobs only work with pans full of cold water
test: Which is true about the gas smell? | It is added on purpose to warn of leaks | It is what makes the gas flame burn a bright blue | It comes from burnt food stuck inside the cooker | It means the gas coming through is extra strong today
test: slow cooker's been off 20 min, can I lift it to the sink? | No, it stays hot a long time; leave it for an adult | Yes, it cools as soon as it is off | Yes, the outside never gets hot | No, it is too heavy to lift
test: The story goes that the microwave oven was invented when an engineer noticed what? | A radar machine had melted a sweet in his pocket | His toaster had blown up | His kettle boiled faster near a radio | His oven door glowed in the dark
exam: Identify the principle by which a domestic microwave oven conveys heat to foodstuffs. | It agitates the water molecules within the food | It heats the surrounding air by means of a flame | It conducts heat upwards from a heated turntable | It irradiates the food with intense visible light
exam: It is given that natural gas possesses no odour of its own. State the reason for which an odour is added prior to supply. | So that any leakage may be readily detected | So that the flame burns with a blue colour | So that the gas is rendered heavier than air | So that the gas cannot be ignited accidentally
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of an induction hob? | A magnetic field causes the vessel itself to become hot | A concealed gas flame heats the glass surface | Heated water circulates beneath the glass | An element heats the glass, which then heats the vessel upon it
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the automatic cut-out of an electric kettle. | Steam deflects a bimetallic strip, which opens the switch | A timer disconnects the supply after a set interval of three minutes | The weight of the water depresses a switch | The handle detects heat and releases the switch
